                <section id="warning-signs" className="scroll-mt-32">
                  <h2 className="text-xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 md:mb-6">Warning Signs of Fraudulent Loan Settlement Agencies</h2>
                  
                  {/* High Contrast Warning Section */}
                  <div className="relative my-12 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-red-200 bg-white shadow-lg">
                    {/* Pulsating background highlight */}
                    <div className="absolute top-0 right-0 w-64 h-64 bg-red-100 opacity-50 rounded-full blur-[80px] animate-pulse pointer-events-none"></div>
                    
                    <div className="bg-red-600 text-white p-6 flex items-center">
                      <svg className="w-8 h-8 mr-3 animate-bounce" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path strokeLinecap="round" strokeLinejoin="round" strokeWidth="2" d="M12 9v2m0 4h.01m-6.938 4h13.856c1.54 0 2.502-1.667 1.732-3L13.732 4c-.77-1.333-2.694-1.333-3.464 0L3.34 16c-.77 1.333.192 3 1.732 3z"></path></svg>
                      <h3 className="text-xl md:text-3xl font-extrabold m-0">Critical Red Flags to Watch Out For</h3>
                    </div>
                    
                    <div className="p-6 md:p-8">
                      <p className="text-gray-700 font-medium mb-6">If an agency exhibits any of the following behaviors, terminate communication immediately. They are highly likely to be fraudulent operations.</p>
                      <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 relative z-10">
                        <div className="flex items-start p-4 bg-red-50/50 rounded-lg border border-red-100 hover:bg-red-50 transition-colors">
                          <div className="w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-red-100 flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 mr-4">
                            <span className="text-red-600 font-bold text-xl">!</span>
                          </div>
                          <div>
                            <h4 className="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Specific Percentage Guarantees</h4>
                            <p className="text-sm text-gray-600">Promising a flat 10% or 20% settlement across the board is illegal and practically impossible to guarantee upfront.</p>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        
                        <div className="flex items-start p-4 bg-red-50/50 rounded-lg border border-red-100 hover:bg-red-50 transition-colors">
                          <div className="w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-red-100 flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 mr-4">
                            <span className="text-red-600 font-bold text-xl">!</span>
                          </div>
                          <div>
                            <h4 className="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Massive Upfront Fees</h4>
                            <p className="text-sm text-gray-600">Requiring you to pay large sums before any negotiation has occurred or any official letter has been produced.</p>
                          </div>
                        </div>

                        <div className="flex items-start p-4 bg-red-50/50 rounded-lg border border-red-100 hover:bg-red-50 transition-colors">
                          <div className="w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-red-100 flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 mr-4">
                            <span className="text-red-600 font-bold text-xl">!</span>
                          </div>
                          <div>
                            <h4 className="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Ignoring Court Summons</h4>
                            <p className="text-sm text-gray-600">A legitimate legal firm will never advise you to ignore official arbitration notices or court summons.</p>
                          </div>
                        </div>

                        <div className="flex items-start p-4 bg-red-50/50 rounded-lg border border-red-100 hover:bg-red-50 transition-colors">
                          <div className="w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-red-100 flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 mr-4">
                            <span className="text-red-600 font-bold text-xl">!</span>
                          </div>
                          <div>
                            <h4 className="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Refusal to Use Escrow</h4>
                            <p className="text-sm text-gray-600">Demanding that all settlement savings be transferred directly to their private corporate account rather than an escrow account.</p>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-8">
                    <div className="bg-white border-t-4 border-red-500 rounded-b-2xl shadow-md p-6 md:p-8">
                      <h3 className="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center">
                        <svg className="w-6 h-6 text-red-500 mr-2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path strokeLinecap="round" strokeLinejoin="round" strokeWidth="2" d="M12 8v4m0 4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>
                        Guarantees of 10-20% Settlements
                      </h3>
                      <p className="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                        The most ubiquitous tactic employed by fraudulent agencies is the false guarantee of absurdly low settlement amounts. They will confidently assert that they can settle your debt for 10 or 20 percent of the outstanding principal, regardless of the bank, the age of the default, or your financial profile. This is a profound and dangerous falsehood.
                      </p>
                      <p className="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
                        Banks are heavily regulated institutions accountable to shareholders and the RBI. They do not arbitrarily write off 90 percent of a debt simply because an agency requests it. The final settlement amount is a complex calculation based on the classification of the loan, the borrower's verifiable insolvency, and the age of the debt. Any agency making ironclad guarantees of specific, massive discounts is utilizing a bait and switch tactic designed solely to extract an upfront enrollment fee before disappearing.
                      </p>
                    </div>

                    <div className="bg-white border-t-4 border-red-500 rounded-b-2xl shadow-md p-6 md:p-8">
                      <h3 className="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center">
                        <svg className="w-6 h-6 text-red-500 mr-2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path strokeLinecap="round" strokeLinejoin="round" strokeWidth="2" d="M12 8c-1.657 0-3 .895-3 2s1.343 2 3 2 3 .895 3 2-1.343 2-3 2m0-8c1.11 0 2.08.402 2.599 1M12 8V7m0 1v8m0 0v1m0-1c-1.11 0-2.08-.402-2.599-1M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>
                        Demanding Upfront Fees Before NOC
                      </h3>
                      <p className="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                        Another critical indicator of fraud is the demand for exorbitant upfront payments disguised as "processing fees" or "legal retainers." While legitimate firms do charge reasonable consultation or onboarding fees to cover initial legal drafting and documentation, fraudulent entities demand amounts running into tens of thousands of rupees before they have even sent a single communication to the bank.
                      </p>
                      <p className="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
                        Once this massive upfront fee is paid, communication typically ceases. They provide evasive answers, blame the bank for delays, and ultimately fail to produce any verifiable settlement letter or NOC. A professional and ethical platform structures its compensation heavily around the success fee, ensuring they are only fully compensated when they successfully deliver a legally binding resolution that genuinely benefits the borrower.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </section>

